          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em Cayley plane
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+plane"} projective plane
            over the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/octonions"} octonions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/octonionic+projective+space"} octonionic projective space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕆
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{O} P^2
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            The Cayley plane can’t be constructed using homogeneous coordinates the way it works for projective planes over
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/division+rings"} division rings
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                One can start with
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝕆
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{O}^2
                and give it the structure of an
                %span.newWikiWord
                  affine plane
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/affine+plane"} ?
                in the obvious way, and then add “points at infinity” in the usual way to obtain a projective plane. This is the most straightforward approach, but as always it has the defect that it makes the line at infinity appear special.
            %li
              %p
                One can consider the space of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 3
                      %mo ×
                      %mn 3
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3\times 3
                matrices over
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝕆
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{O}
                that are “Hermitian” and idempotent, hence can be imagined as “projections onto dimension-1 subspaces of
                = succeed "\u201D." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi 𝕆
                          %mn 3
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{O}^3
            %li
              %p
                Writing out the components of such a matrix explicitly, one obtains a
                %em Veronese vector
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 3
                      %mo ;
                      %msub
                        %mi ξ
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi ξ
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi ξ
                        %mn 3
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x_1,x_2,x_3;\xi_1,\xi_2,\xi_3)
                where
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi i
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi 𝕆
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_i\in \mathbb{O}
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ξ
                          %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi ℝ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \xi_i\in \mathbb{R}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ξ
                        %mi i
                      %mover
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi x
                            %mi i
                        %mo ¯
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi j
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \xi_i \overline{x_i} = x_j x_k
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi i
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                        %mn 2
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi ξ
                        %mi j
                      %msub
                        %mi ξ
                        %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Vert x_i\Vert^2 = \xi_j \xi_k
                for all cyclic permutations
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi i
                      %mo ,
                      %mi j
                      %mo ,
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (i,j,k)
                of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 2
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 3
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (1,2,3)
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℝ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#general General
          %p
            The octonionic projective plane is a non-Desarguesian plane, that is,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Desargues%27+theorem"} Desargues' theorem
            does not hold. See
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+plane"} projective plane
          %h3#isometries Isometries
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F4"} F4
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isometry+group"} isometry group
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝕆
                      %msup
                        %mi P
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{O} P^2
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stabilizer"} stabilizer
              of a point being
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Spin%289%29"} Spin(9)
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝕆
                      %msup
                        %mi P
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ≅
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mn 4
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi Spin
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 9
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{O} P^2 \cong F_4/Spin(9)
          %h3#cell_structure Cell structure
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              There is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphism"} homeomorphism
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕆
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mn 2
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ≃
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mn 16
                    %munder
                      %mo ∪
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi h
                          %mi 𝕆
                    %mi 𝕆
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathbb{O}P^2 \,\simeq\, D^{16} \underset{h_{\mathbb{O}}}{\cup} \mathbb{O}P^1
            %p
              between the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/octonionic+projective+plane"} octonionic projective plane
              and the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/attaching+space"} attaching space
              obtained from the
